1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,509
The gas in a cooking cylinder.

2
00:00:02,509 --> 00:00:04,217
The petrol in a scooter.

3
00:00:04,217 --> 00:00:05,730
The wax in a candle.

4
00:00:06,130 --> 00:00:07,558
They look nothing alike.

5
00:00:07,558 --> 00:00:11,209
But all three are made of just
two elements, carbon and hydrogen.

6
00:00:11,659 --> 00:00:13,454
Aarav oils his bicycle chain.

7
00:00:13,454 --> 00:00:15,670
That oil comes from the same family.

8
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:17,391
Welcome to a new chapter.

9
00:00:17,391 --> 00:00:18,291
Hydrocarbons.

10
00:00:18,623 --> 00:00:21,758
By the end, you will build these
molecules in four different ways.

11
00:00:22,408 --> 00:00:26,459
A hydrocarbon is a compound
of only carbon and hydrogen.

12
00:00:26,809 --> 00:00:30,897
If every carbon uses only single
bonds, the molecule is full.

13
00:00:30,897 --> 00:00:32,910
It cannot take more hydrogen.

14
00:00:32,910 --> 00:00:34,461
We call it saturated.

15
00:00:34,461 --> 00:00:35,800
These are alkanes.

16
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:38,361
A double bond gives an alkene.

17
00:00:38,361 --> 00:00:40,522
A triple bond gives an alkyne.

18
00:00:40,522 --> 00:00:44,148
They can take more hydrogen,
so they are unsaturated.

19
00:00:44,548 --> 00:00:46,629
Carbons in a ring make a cycloalkane.

20
00:00:46,629 --> 00:00:49,760
And the special ring of benzene
makes aromatic hydrocarbons.

21
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:52,668
Every alkane follows one rule.

22
00:00:52,668 --> 00:00:54,605
C n H two n plus two.

23
00:00:54,955 --> 00:00:56,490
One carbon, four hydrogens.

24
00:00:56,490 --> 00:00:57,390
Methane.

25
00:00:57,052 --> 00:00:58,588
Two carbons, six hydrogens.

26
00:00:58,588 --> 00:00:59,488
Ethane.

27
00:00:59,110 --> 00:01:00,361
Three carbons, eight.

28
00:01:00,361 --> 00:01:01,261
Propane.

29
00:01:01,323 --> 00:01:03,141
Alkanes were once called paraffins.

30
00:01:03,141 --> 00:01:04,596
That means little affinity.

31
00:01:04,596 --> 00:01:06,146
They are not keen to react.

32
00:01:06,746 --> 00:01:08,637
Now, how do we make them?

33
00:01:08,637 --> 00:01:10,413
Method one, add hydrogen.

34
00:01:10,413 --> 00:01:12,595
Let's watch on our molecule bench.

35
00:01:12,945 --> 00:01:14,795
Ethene has a double bond.

36
00:01:14,795 --> 00:01:19,473
Hydrogen gas meets a metal catalyst,
platinum, palladium, or nickel.

37
00:01:19,473 --> 00:01:23,123
A catalyst speeds up a
reaction without being used up.

38
00:01:23,523 --> 00:01:27,879
On the metal surface, the hydrogen
molecule splits into two atoms.

39
00:01:28,279 --> 00:01:29,804
The double bond opens.

40
00:01:29,804 --> 00:01:31,818
One hydrogen joins each carbon.

41
00:01:32,218 --> 00:01:34,052
Now every bond is single.

42
00:01:34,052 --> 00:01:35,824
Ethene has become ethane.

43
00:01:35,824 --> 00:01:38,952
Platinum and palladium
work at room temperature.

44
00:01:38,952 --> 00:01:41,408
Nickel needs more heat and pressure.

45
00:01:42,008 --> 00:01:44,434
Method two starts from alkyl halides.

46
00:01:44,434 --> 00:01:48,852
An alkyl halide is an alkane with
one hydrogen swapped for a halogen.

47
00:01:49,202 --> 00:01:53,070
With zinc and dilute hydrochloric acid,
the halogen is replaced by hydrogen.

48
00:01:53,070 --> 00:01:54,602
Chloromethane becomes methane.

49
00:01:54,602 --> 00:01:56,516
This works for all except fluorides.

50
00:01:56,916 --> 00:01:58,801
The Wurtz reaction joins two pieces.

51
00:01:58,801 --> 00:02:01,162
Two bromomethane, two
sodium, in dry ether.

52
00:02:01,562 --> 00:02:04,628
Sodium pulls off both
bromines as sodium bromide.

53
00:02:04,628 --> 00:02:06,460
The two methyl pieces join.

54
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:07,360
Ethane.

55
00:02:07,096 --> 00:02:08,774
The carbon count doubles.

56
00:02:09,374 --> 00:02:12,027
Method three starts from carboxylic acids.

57
00:02:12,027 --> 00:02:14,339
Heat sodium acetate with soda lime.

58
00:02:14,339 --> 00:02:17,805
Soda lime is sodium hydroxide
mixed with calcium oxide.

59
00:02:18,155 --> 00:02:21,630
The carbon dioxide part
leaves as sodium carbonate.

60
00:02:21,630 --> 00:02:22,933
Methane is left.

61
00:02:22,933 --> 00:02:24,236
One carbon less.

62
00:02:24,236 --> 00:02:25,963
This is decarboxylation.

63
00:02:26,563 --> 00:02:29,040
Method four is Kolbe's electrolysis.

64
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:32,399
Pass electricity through
sodium acetate in water.

65
00:02:32,799 --> 00:02:37,091
At the positive electrode, each
acetate ion loses an electron.

66
00:02:37,091 --> 00:02:40,525
It breaks into carbon
dioxide and a methyl piece.

67
00:02:40,925 --> 00:02:43,559
Two methyl pieces join to make ethane.

68
00:02:43,559 --> 00:02:46,397
Hydrogen gas forms at the other electrode.

69
00:02:46,397 --> 00:02:48,877
Methane can never be made this way.

70
00:02:49,477 --> 00:02:50,596
Compare two methods.

71
00:02:50,596 --> 00:02:51,871
Wurtz with bromomethane.

72
00:02:51,871 --> 00:02:53,639
One carbon in, two carbons out.

73
00:02:53,639 --> 00:02:54,539
Doubled.

74
00:02:54,594 --> 00:02:57,046
Decarboxylation of sodium acetate.

75
00:02:57,046 --> 00:02:59,576
Two carbons in, one carbon out.

76
00:02:59,576 --> 00:03:00,494
One less.

77
00:03:00,994 --> 00:03:02,070
Pause and think.

78
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:03,190
You need methane.

79
00:03:03,190 --> 00:03:04,791
Wurtz, or decarboxylation?

80
00:03:07,991 --> 00:03:10,143
Decarboxylation of sodium acetate.

81
00:03:10,143 --> 00:03:15,045
Wurtz and Kolbe always join two pieces,
so the smallest they give is ethane.

82
00:03:15,695 --> 00:03:16,629
Remember this.

83
00:03:16,629 --> 00:03:19,682
Count the carbons in, and
you know the carbons out.

84
00:03:20,382 --> 00:03:22,072
Now, one worked example.

85
00:03:22,372 --> 00:03:24,027
Which alkane forms in each case?

86
00:03:24,027 --> 00:03:25,172
Propene with hydrogen.

87
00:03:25,172 --> 00:03:26,280
Bromoethane by Wurtz.

88
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:27,927
Sodium propanoate with soda lime.

89
00:03:28,427 --> 00:03:29,981
Propene plus hydrogen.

90
00:03:29,981 --> 00:03:31,748
Three carbons stay three.

91
00:03:31,748 --> 00:03:32,648
Propane.

92
00:03:33,219 --> 00:03:34,325
Bromoethane by Wurtz.

93
00:03:34,325 --> 00:03:35,225
Two plus two.

94
00:03:35,131 --> 00:03:36,031
Butane.

95
00:03:36,374 --> 00:03:38,150
Sodium propanoate with soda lime.

96
00:03:38,150 --> 00:03:39,144
Three minus one.

97
00:03:39,144 --> 00:03:40,044
Ethane.

98
00:03:40,427 --> 00:03:41,888
Now, the exam trap.

99
00:03:42,238 --> 00:03:45,036
Students think bromomethane and
bromoethane together, by Wurtz, give only

100
00:03:45,036 --> 00:03:45,936
propane.

101
00:03:45,877 --> 00:03:48,125
But the pieces join in every way.

102
00:03:48,125 --> 00:03:51,797
Methyl with methyl, methyl
with ethyl, ethyl with ethyl.

103
00:03:51,797 --> 00:03:54,674
You get ethane, propane and butane, mixed.

104
00:03:55,324 --> 00:03:56,719
Where is this asked?

105
00:03:56,719 --> 00:03:59,078
Boards ask for the reactions by name.

106
00:03:59,078 --> 00:04:03,216
Entrance tests ask which product
forms, or which alkane a method cannot

107
00:04:03,216 --> 00:04:04,116
make.

108
00:04:04,341 --> 00:04:05,358
Exam level now.

109
00:04:05,358 --> 00:04:09,205
A water solution of sodium propanoate
is electrolysed by Kolbe's method.

110
00:04:09,205 --> 00:04:11,327
Which hydrocarbon forms at the anode?

111
00:04:11,327 --> 00:04:13,569
Butane, propane, ethane, or hexane?

112
00:04:13,569 --> 00:04:14,630
Pause and solve.

113
00:04:20,030 --> 00:04:21,569
The answer is A.

114
00:04:21,569 --> 00:04:22,469
Butane.

115
00:04:22,856 --> 00:04:26,565
Propanoate loses an electron,
then loses carbon dioxide.

116
00:04:26,565 --> 00:04:29,508
An ethyl piece is left, with two carbons.

117
00:04:30,008 --> 00:04:31,803
Two ethyl pieces join.

118
00:04:31,803 --> 00:04:33,609
Two plus two is four.

119
00:04:33,609 --> 00:04:34,509
Butane.

120
00:04:34,854 --> 00:04:37,838
C, ethane, comes from
soda lime, not Kolbe.

121
00:04:37,838 --> 00:04:40,100
D forgot that carbon dioxide leaves.

122
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:42,240
Here is your recap.

123
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:43,823
Screenshot this card.

124
00:04:47,023 --> 00:04:49,209
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

125
00:04:49,609 --> 00:04:50,509
One.

126
00:04:49,999 --> 00:04:52,004
What is the general formula of alkanes?

127
00:04:52,804 --> 00:04:53,704
Two.

128
00:04:53,247 --> 00:04:56,063
Which method gives one
carbon less than the acid?

129
00:04:56,863 --> 00:04:57,763
Three.

130
00:04:57,300 --> 00:04:59,251
Why can Kolbe's method never make methane?

131
00:05:00,151 --> 00:05:04,387
Next time: why does cooking gas burn,
and what does chlorine do to methane

132
00:05:04,387 --> 00:05:05,287
in sunlight?

133
00:05:05,245 --> 00:05:07,474
We find out in properties of alkanes.
